#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184045 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184344 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "240f83b9-6c11-53d6-a193-708221b59741" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the First Principal Component From a Scatter Plot"}] :question/id #uuid "db398e4a-388c-57d0-bc1b-9fb65d66707d" :question/math-academy-id "e-21882" :question/problem "![](images/31/31cc010ebe0a36d7c94ff0e5dd33c6c09a6d13c88f8c4696e32ce620dd037b00.png)\n\n \n\nThe scatter plot for a sample of paired observations is shown above. What vectors correspond to the direction of the first principal component of the data?" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the *first principal component* of the data corresponds to the direction in which the data varies the most (the direction where we have the maximum variance).\n\nIn our diagram, the direction where the variance changes the most corresponds (approximately) to the vector $\\mathbf{a},$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/9d/9daed2ca60f9277d0b4b02c3c013446dc696960fc51065a83cd1c213a5afefde.png)\n\n \n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$\\mathbf{a}$ only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184046 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184345 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f6314eaa-c493-5b5e-8488-3575762a00e0" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Second Principal Component From a Scatter Plot"}] :question/id #uuid "e71cf4f3-bd45-520b-86e6-22b348516139" :question/math-academy-id "e-21885" :question/problem "![](images/9c/9c625bd3abc4fd02cf26119315911b24d06f8b31aa2977cf1571a8741ce091dd.png)\n\n   \n\nThe scatter plot for a sample of paired observations is shown above. What vectors correspond to the direction of the second principal component of the data?" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the *first principal component* of the data corresponds to the direction in which the data varies the most (the direction where we have the maximum variance).\n\nThe *second principal component* of the data corresponds to the direction perpendicular to the first principal component in which the data varies the most.\n\nIn our diagram, the direction where the variance changes the most corresponds (approximately) to the vector $\\mathbf{b},$ as shown below. So, $\\mathbf{b}$ defines the direction of the first principal component.\n\n![](images/2b/2b4539ab239ecf0e54d5e828079469b928dd9201ae5aa913db13104bf1ed1796.png)\n\n   \n\nNext, notice that in the 2D case, we have only one direction perpendicular to the first principal component. And this direction corresponds to the vector $\\mathbf{c},$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/a3/a3d1e84cc1a20da39af058b422ea7860a11eb97c0afa59be752b0ca3ed0a14be.png)\n\n  \n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$\\mathbf{c}$ only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184047 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184346 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "de2ed4a0-7a12-5b55-adfc-32f705af4e99" :knowledge-point/title "Representing Principal Components as Linear Combinations"}] :question/id #uuid "10f2c954-d70e-56eb-b981-ad393a620521" :question/math-academy-id "e-21886" :question/problem "A geologist has measured two specific features, $x_1$ and $x_2,$ for each rock among a sample of rocks she is studying. The collected data was stored in a standardized observation matrix, where each row contains both features for a particular rock.\nThe first and second principal components of the data are approximately \n![](images/5d/5d1c7f2402d1142e6d7ab3787a2459e19607a5aaf97761c3dff917a5a74deb42.svg)\nBy combining the two measured features linearly, the geologist wants to construct a single feature that preserves the maximum amount of variance of the collected data. Which of the following could be the best candidate for the new feature?" :question/worked-solution "Recall the direction in which the data varies the most (the direction where we have the maximum variance) corresponds to the *first principal component* of the data.\n\n![](images/97/97a46ed0c33579b24a5c43003b908ad7b04c78a3e5b5486d4bdc5d0fe2bb3bee.png)\n\n  \n\nLet  \n\n![](images/a8/a8013014e445cf1099127a588812b42312c7ce7d307b23a21b9b496509949e5a.svg)\n\nbe the matrix of principal components. Then, applying the change of variables $Y = XV,$ we can convert our observation matrix $X$ into a new matrix $Y$ of (linearly) transformed observations such that\n\n- the covariance matrix of $Y$ is diagonal (meaning that the new features $y_1$ and $y_2$ are uncorrelated), and\n\n- the features $y_1$ and $y_2$ are arranged in order of decreasing variance (meaning that most of the variance is concentrated in $y_1$).\n\nApplying our change of variables to a single observation ![](images/88/888217508e2871244251b77c465094fa6e581ccc36b92aa1ebc62a98f8a25654.svg) we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]V \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-0.447 & -0.894 \\\\ 0.894 & -0.447\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-0.447{x}_{1}+0.894{x}_{2} & -0.894{x}_{1}-0.447{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have that $y_1 = -0.447 x_1 + 0.894 x_2$ will contain most of the variance of the data. \n\nTherefore, the best candidate for the new single feature is\n\n$$\ny_1 = -0.447 x_1 + 0.894 x_2.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184512 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184344 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "240f83b9-6c11-53d6-a193-708221b59741" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the First Principal Component From a Scatter Plot"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ef33ca28-44f3-5f21-ba28-ef1e6a5cf96a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$b$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186181251} {:answer/id #uuid "2bac4778-cc57-5211-8e5c-44d351aa6b92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$c$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 175