 only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184751 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184346 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "de2ed4a0-7a12-5b55-adfc-32f705af4e99" :knowledge-point/title "Representing Principal Components as Linear Combinations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "26c64a9d-85b7-54d4-85e2-b8629081ee3c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.743{x}_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513640} {:answer/id #uuid "528de0bd-c0e1-5533-b0aa-55c1f79b9edd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.743{x}_{1}-0.669{x}_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513761} {:answer/id #uuid "7ba53d25-2ed0-5535-8d49-9d22d605ebad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.669{x}_{1}+0.743{x}_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513857} {:answer/id #uuid "9d37f248-ad0b-5895-b853-5ba2f3d76a2d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.669({x}_{1}+{x}_{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513938} {:answer/id #uuid "c743983c-ef48-5d67-86bf-908e9cc1b4b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.669{x}_{1}-0.743{x}_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514055}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7ba53d25-2ed0-5535-8d49-9d22d605ebad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.669{x}_{1}+0.743{x}_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513857} :answer-field/id #uuid "850a97c7-43d9-546d-8101-67fdfb947421" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183115}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e9148622-4d91-5cc0-b3f6-f75f09b1b9e4" :question/math-academy-id "q-343703" :question/problem "An engineer measured two particular features, ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2},$ for each road bridge in a city. The collected data was stored in a standardized observation matrix, where each row contains both features for a particular bridge. \nThe first and second principal components of the data are approximately\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}≈[\\begin{aligned}0.669 \\\\ 0.743\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{2}≈[\\begin{aligned}0.743 \\\\ -0.669\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nBy combining the two measured features linearly, the engineer wants to construct a single feature that preserves the maximum amount of variance of the collected data. Which of the following could be the best candidate for the new feature?" :question/worked-solution "Recall the direction in which the data varies the most (the direction where we have the maximum variance) corresponds to the *first principal component* of the data.\n\n![](images/c4/c47327219f03934832e62176db1441a94d9a78edda2f0d398a63b2942ec6a845.png)\n\n  \n\nLet  \n\n$$\nV=[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ {v}_{1} & {v}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0.669 & 0.743 \\\\ 0.743 & -0.669\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nbe the matrix of principal components. \n\nThen, applying the change of variables $Y=XV,$ we can convert our observation matrix $X$ into a new matrix $Y$ of (linearly) transformed observations such that\n\n- the covariance matrix of $Y$ is diagonal (meaning that the new features ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are uncorrelated), and\n\n- the features ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are arranged in order of decreasing variance (meaning that most of the variance is concentrated in ${y}_{1}$).\n\nApplying our change of variables to a single observation $x=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]V \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0.669 & 0.743 \\\\ 0.743 & -0.669\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0.669{x}_{1}+0.743{x}_{2} & 0.743{x}_{1}-0.669{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have that ${y}_{1}=0.669{x}_{1}+0.743{x}_{2}$ will contain most of the variance of the data. \n\nTherefore, the best candidate for the new single feature is\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}=0.669{x}_{1}+0.743{x}_{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514467 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184346 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "de2ed4a0-7a12-5b55-adfc-32f705af4e99" :knowledge-point/title "Representing Principal Components as Linear Combinations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07a26175-d1b6-5968-8d75-e79f81b8f4e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.667({x}_{1}+{x}_{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513551} {:answer/id #uuid "241fe0b4-e84c-5b91-973c-3e2ccbadf541" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.667{x}_{1}+0.745{x}_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513633} {:answer/id #uuid "7cd3bf57-87be-5a0e-a26f-d05223e219cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.745{x}_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513858} {:answer/id #uuid "a2558652-2910-5d66-aae1-99df04a43b38" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.745{x}_{1}+0.667{x}_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513951} {:answer/id #uuid "e3f09a11-3e11-526e-86f4-d3c7a493f6c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.667{x}_{1}-0.745{x}_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514133}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "241fe0b4-e84c-5b91-973c-3e2ccbadf541" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.667{x}_{1}+0.745{x}_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513633} :answer-field/id #uuid "8c4e8c66-d4aa-5c56-a392-1d26ce6b88cb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514295}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "dd2cd7be-06ce-5ffb-8860-cd5d1eb5c130" :question/math-academy-id "q-344073" :question/problem "A meteorologist measured two particular features, ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2},$ at various locations throughout California. The collected data was stored in a standardized observation matrix, where each row contains both features for a particular location. \nThe first and second principal components of the data are approximately\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}≈[\\begin{aligned}0.667 \\\\ 0.745\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{2}≈[\\begin{aligned}-0.745 \\\\ 0.667\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nBy combining the two measured features linearly, the meteorologist wants to construct a single feature that preserves the maximum amount of variance of the collected data. Which of the following could be the best candidate for the new feature?" :question/worked-solution "Recall the direction in which the data varies the most (the direction where we have the maximum variance) corresponds to the *first principal component* of the data.\n\n![](images/f7/f7c823606bbbe77326819df5d487b5e0019dd7439b46124547f71681b9484f6d.png)\n\n \n\nLet  \n\n$$\nV=[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ {v}_{1} & {v}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0.667 & -0.745 \\\\ 0.745 & 0.667\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nbe the matrix of principal components. \n\nThen, applying the change of variables $Y=XV,$ we can convert our observation matrix $X$ into a new matrix $Y$ of (linearly) transformed observations such that\n\n- the covariance matrix of $Y$ is diagonal (meaning that the new features ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are uncorrelated), and\n\n- the features ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are arranged in order of decreasing variance (meaning that most of the variance is concentrated in ${y}_{1}$).\n\nApplying our change of variables to a single observation $x=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]V \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0.667 & -0.745 \\\\ 0.745 & 0.667\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0.667{x}_{1}+0.745{x}_{2} & -0.745{x}_{1}+0.667{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have that ${y}_{1}=0.667{x}_{1}+0.745{x}_{2}$ will contain most of the variance of the data. \n\nTherefore, the best candidate for the new single feature is\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}=0.667{x}_{1}+0.745{x}_{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514430 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184346 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "de2ed4a0-7a12-5b55-adfc-32f705af4e99" :knowledge-point/title "Representing Principal Components as Linear Combinations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4401a834-4408-55a4-bed9-f955775055e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.890{x}_{1}-0.456{x}_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513712} {:answer/id #uuid "9816a44e-fa5d-597c-a8ab-5c65208603f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.456({x}_{1}+{x}_{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513926} {:answer/id #uuid "cefe2cd0-91db-55d5-af14-8affe3de1778" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.890{x}_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514072} {:answer/id #uuid "d16e9923-2eb9-5f81-90f0-c065974959cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.456{x}_{1}-0.890{x}_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514076} {:answer/id #uuid "e1fd7d42-929f-5eaf-a861-ae3e9d553b92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.456{x}_{1}+0.890{x}_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514124}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e1fd7d42-929f-5eaf-a861-ae3e9d553b92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.456{x}_{1}+0.890{x}_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514124} :answer-field/id #uuid "e68ffcc9-1fd2-5b21-96cd-20a43203877f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514334}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7eb658cd-5383-5f28-8b76-9d708b2f6422" :question/math-academy-id "q-344079" :question/problem "A physicist measured two particular features, ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2},$ for each oscillation produced when a mass hanging on a spring is displaced and then released. The collected data was stored in a standardized observation matrix, where each row contains both features for a particular oscillation. \nThe first and second principal components of the data are approximately\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}≈[\\begin{aligned}0.456 \\\\ 0.890\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{2}≈[\\begin{aligned}0.890 \\\\ -0.456\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nBy combining the two measured features linearly, the physicist wants to construct a single feature that preserves the maximum amount of variance of the collected data. Which of the following could be the best candidate for the new feature?" :question/worked-solution "Recall the direction in which the data varies the most (the direction where we have the maximum variance) corresponds to the *first principal component* of the data.\n\n![](images/f0/f0f0623703d1a3767a7c3efd275e977c6933465faeb761991f00da705bac4a2c.png)\n\n \n\nLet  \n\n$$\nV=[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ {v}_{1} & {v}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0.456 & 0.890 \\\\ 0.890 & -0.456\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nbe the matrix of principal components. \n\nThen, applying the change of variables $Y=XV,$ we can convert our observation matrix $X$ into a new matrix $Y$ of (linearly) transformed observations such that\n\n- the covariance matrix of $Y$ is diagonal (meaning that the new features ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are uncorrelated), and\n\n- the features ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are arranged in order of decreasing variance (meaning that most of the variance is concentrated in ${y}_{1}$).\n\nApplying our change of variables to a single observation $x=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]V \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0.456 & 0.890 \\\\ 0.890 & -0.456\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0.456{x}_{1}+0.890{x}_{2} & 0.890{x}_{1}-0.456{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have that ${y}_{1}=0.456{x}_{1}+0.890{x}_{2}$ will contain most of the variance of the data. \n\nTherefore, the best candidate for the new single feature is\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}=0.456{x}_{1}+0.890{x}_{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514378 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184346 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "de2ed4a0-7a12-5b55-adfc-32f705af4e99" :knowledge-point/title "Representing Principal Components as Linear Combinations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "003afbd0-71df-5851-ac97-766aac2021f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.707{x}_{1}+0.707{x}_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513526} {:answer/id #uuid "0323a014-9e20-5787-b0d1-3bef4eb4e870" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.707{x}_{1}-0.707{x}_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513536} {:answer/id #uuid "4aded3ac-f955-5149-a241-69be81cf8871" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.707({x}_{1}+{x}_{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513735} {:answer/id #uuid "59762be5-4665-590a-a8b0-f439efc3af1e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.707{x}_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513772} {:answer/id #uuid "8b043862-3c88-5ffc-9063-4108680f5b61" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.707{x}_{1}-0.707{x}_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513893}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0323a014-9e20-5787-b0d1-3bef4eb4e870" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.707{x}_{1}-0.707{x}_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513536} :answer-field/id #uuid "e66bcdbe-e3e0-5f78-be6e-bc00cded069c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514333}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2381e2c6-90e5-5e9a-8022-1f6b70cb4895" :question/math-academy-id "q-344084" :question/problem "A neuroscientist measured two specific features, ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2},$ for each brain image of a group of patients he is studying. The collected data was stored in a standardized observation matrix, where each row contains both features for a particular brain image. \nThe first and second principal components of the data are approximately\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}≈[\\begin{aligned}-0.707 \\\\ -0.707\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{2}≈[\\begin{aligned}-0.707 \\\\ 0.707\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nBy combining the two measured features linearly, the neuroscientist wants to construct a single feature that preserves the maximum amount of variance of the collected data. Which of the following could be the best candidate for the new feature?" :question/worked-solution "Recall the direction in which the data varies the most (the direction where we have the maximum variance) corresponds to the *first principal component* of the data.\n\n![](images/70/70a37afaaad54e3f384ad446ab2cf57bc0f21ffa8f6986f01841d9f4041f531c.png)\n\n  \n\nLet  \n\n$$\nV=[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ {v}_{1} & {v}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-0.707 & -0.707 \\\\ -0.707 & 0.707\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nbe the matrix of principal components. \n\nThen, applying the change of variables $Y=XV,$ we can convert our observation matrix $X$ into a new matrix $Y$ of (linearly) transformed observations such that\n\n- the covariance matrix of $Y$ is diagonal (meaning that the new features ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are uncorrelated), and\n\n- the features ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are arranged in order of decreasing variance (meaning that most of the variance is concentrated in ${y}_{1}$).\n\nApplying our change of variables to a single observation $x=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]V \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-0.707 & -0.707 \\\\ -0.707 & 0.707\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-0.707{x}_{1}-0.707{x}_{2} & -0.707{x}_{1}+0.707{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have that ${y}_{1}=-0.707{x}_{1}-0.707{x}_{2}$ will contain most of the variance of the data. \n\nTherefore, the best candidate for the new single feature is\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}=-0.707{x}_{1}-0.707{x}_{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514415 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184346 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "de2ed4a0-7a12-5b55-adfc-32f705af4e99" :knowledge-point/title "Representing Principal Components as Linear Combinations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c54fdba-b389-5b54-9720-959b5f18971d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.832{x}_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513561} {:answer/id #uuid "0e524028-ca98-575f-a980-f0530848a15a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.555{x}_{1}+0.832{x}_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513565} {:answer/id #uuid "1e4d4fb4-8dff-5794-a3cc-51edd2d40060" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.832{x}_{1}+0.555{x}_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513610} {:answer/id #uuid "3ce71e51-1c0f-564c-a052-a0ab97df49d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.555{x}_{1}-0.832{x}_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513690} {:answer/id #uuid "d2231053-6aed-5e86-8317-202410bd2a61" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.555({x}_{1}+{x}_{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514080}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0e524028-ca98-575f-a980-f0530848a15a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.555{x}_{1}+0.832{x}_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513565} :answer-field/id #uuid "1cab87af-ca27-5d76-8fd5-6124194c1d37" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514226}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "68cd1b00-9920-5784-9707-6f682f05d636" :question/math-academy-id "q-344087" :question/problem "A physicist measured two particular features, ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2},$ for each electron released in an atomic experiment. The collected data was stored in a standardized observation matrix, where each row contains both features for a particular electron. \nThe first and second principal components of the data are approximately\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}≈[\\begin{aligned}-0.555 \\\\ 0.832\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{2}≈[\\begin{aligned}0.832 \\\\ 0.555\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nBy combining the two measured features linearly, the physicist wants to construct a single feature that preserves the maximum amount of variance of the collected data. Which of the following could be the best candidate for the new feature?" :question/worked-solution "Recall the direction in which the data varies the most (the direction where we have the maximum variance) corresponds to the *first principal component* of the data.\n\n![](images/17/171a9c86bf510c2ee5b65325d6f8939c65b5118286057934f91a053f75faf82f.png)\n\n  \n\nLet  \n\n$$\nV=[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ {v}_{1} & {v}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-0.555 & 0.832 \\\\ 0.832 & 0.555\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nbe the matrix of principal components. \n\nThen, applying the change of variables $Y=XV,$ we can convert our observation matrix $X$ into a new matrix $Y$ of (linearly) transformed observations such that\n\n- the covariance matrix of $Y$ is diagonal (meaning that the new features ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are uncorrelated), and\n\n- the features ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are arranged in order of decreasing variance (meaning that most of the variance is concentrated in ${y}_{1}$).\n\nApplying our change of variables to a single observation $x=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]V \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-0.555 & 0.832 \\\\ 0.832 & 0.555\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-0.555{x}_{1}+0.832{x}_{2} & 0.832{x}_{1}+0.555{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have that ${y}_{1}=-0.555{x}_{1}+0.832{x}_{2}$ will contain most of the variance of the data. \n\nTherefore, the best candidate for the new single feature is\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}=-0.555{x}_{1}+0.832{x}_{2}.\n$$"}]}
